🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗰𝗸 𝗶𝗻 𝗜𝗺𝗮𝗴𝗲𝗣𝘂𝗹𝗹𝗕𝗮𝗰𝗸𝗢𝗳𝗳 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your Kubernetes Pod is stuck in "ImagePullBackOff". The application is not starting because Kubernetes cannot pull the container image. How would you troubleshoot this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝘂𝘀

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events section for the exact image-pull error.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗜𝗺𝗮𝗴𝗲 𝗡𝗮𝗺𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗧𝗮𝗴

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

Check the image repository, name, tag, and registry URL.

A typo or a tag that does not exist can prevent Kubernetes from pulling the image.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗥𝗲𝗴𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘆 𝗔𝗰𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀

If the image is stored in a private registry, verify the image-pull credentials and "imagePullSecrets" configuration.

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml
kubectl get secrets -n <namespace>

Remember: the referenced Secret must exist in the same namespace as the Pod.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗥𝗲𝗴𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘆 𝗔𝘃𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗮𝗯𝗶𝗹𝗶𝘁𝘆

Check whether the worker node can reach the container registry. Investigate DNS resolution, firewall rules, proxy settings, and registry outages if the events indicate connectivity problems.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗜𝗔𝗠 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗥𝗲𝗴𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘆 𝗣𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

For cloud registries, verify that the node or workload identity has the required permissions to retrieve the image. The exact setup depends on the registry and cluster platform.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗙𝗶𝘅 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆

Correct the image reference, credentials, permissions, or connectivity issue identified in the Events. Then monitor the Pod:

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -w

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

"ImagePullBackOff" means Kubernetes is backing off and retrying after an image-pull failure. Always identify the actual pull error before changing the configuration.
